WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
This is the GitHub Pages deployment status. Use this URL to open the public project page.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.500
The platform is positioned for China vehicle carbon-emission policy simulation.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:14.500
This section summarizes the model capability and project value.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.500
The demo section embeds a screen recording that shows the full workflow from the GitHub Pages page to the local application.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.000
It also introduces deployment options, typical result displays, and workflow traceability.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The project is currently presented as a public preview, with the full model planned for later release.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.500
After the full simulation model and environment configuration are released, users can download them locally.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.500
Users can run the local web interface with the command python web_app.py.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:25.500
After the service starts successfully,

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.000
users can open a browser and enter http://127.0.0.1:8765 to access the simulation interface.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:32.000
On the simulation interface, the left side contains scenario settings, and the right side shows model output summaries.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Users can modify parameters such as the final analysis year, carbon-emission standard, credit trading efficiency, and transition constraints.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Multiprocessing simulation can be enabled here when needed.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Custom configuration files can be uploaded in the Policy and Input Files dropdown.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.000
More advanced parameters can also be configured.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.000
The run history panel shows all local simulation results.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.000
The right side displays key data and figures from the simulation outputs.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.000
The output file list records all data, charts, and logs generated by each run for later review.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:21.500
After returning to the configuration area and confirming the parameters and data files, click Run Scenario to create a new simulation task.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:39.000
The system creates an independent task and displays the task ID, run status, and real-time log window.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.633
The log keeps updating the model execution process, helping track progress, locate issues, and wait for output completion.
